# wfg-log 1
# fixture verified-chain — a closed loop, reproduced with nothing on the other
# end of it.
#
# The seventh replay fixture, and the one that shows why a read-back had to be a
# COMMAND rather than a return value. A verified cue asks a device what a value
# is and waits for the answer; the asking happens on a thread of its own, over
# HTTP, to a box that is not here. None of that can be replayed.
#
# What can be replayed is the ANSWER, because §3.15 makes it an event: somebody
# else's machine said something, so it enters the model as a logged command
# applied on the tick it arrived. A replay re-injects it and the cue reaches the
# same verdict on the same tick, with no network, no device and no thread. A
# probe that had handed its answer straight to the Runner would have made the
# verified cue the one thing in the engine that could not be replayed.
#
# GO on a cue that waits to be told. Nothing has happened yet: the value is in
# the tree, the datagram is queued, and the run is playing while it waits.
A 40 0 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 cue.fire s:"P9XKC2WR" s:"H2K4M6NP"
#
# The desk answered, and agreed. Origin `mount:<id>` rather than `engine`,
# because this is not Go.dot reporting about itself - it is a different machine
# being quoted, and a log that could not tell those apart would be a log that
# could not say whose fault anything was.
#
# The value is a float32 because the NODE is: the client coerced what the desk
# said to the type the mounted description declares. Without that a reply of `1`
# would be an integer, would not compare equal to the float that was written,
# and a device doing exactly what it was told would be recorded as disagreeing.
A 55 1 mount:K3PV7WRB mount.readback s:"K3PV7WRB" s:"/desk/fader" f:1
A 56 2 engine run.ended s:"H2K4M6NP"
#
# THE NEXT CUE IN THE CHAIN, which is the whole point of `verified`: it was
# written knowing the fader had actually arrived, rather than hoping. A sequence
# built this way stops when a device stops, instead of carrying on into a scene
# whose desk never heard the first instruction.
A 100 3 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 cue.fire s:"V3XY5Z7B" s:"Q6RS8TW2"
#
# And this time the desk disagreed. It is there, it is listening, and it is not
# doing what it was told - a clipped range, a mode that ignores the parameter, a
# channel somebody re-patched at the weekend.
#
# `disagreed` and not `timeout`, and the distinction is the reason both words
# exist: one sends somebody to look at the device, the other to look at the
# network. A cue that reported "failed" and stopped there would send them to
# look at both, at half past seven, with an audience in.
A 118 4 mount:K3PV7WRB mount.readback s:"K3PV7WRB" s:"/desk/scene" i:3
A 119 5 engine run.failed s:"Q6RS8TW2" s:"disagreed"
#
# The same cue again, and this time nothing answers at all. There is no record
# of the silence, deliberately: a device that did not reply has told us nothing,
# and a log of what did not happen would be a log of the network rather than of
# the show. What IS a record is the cue running out of patience - two seconds,
# a hundred ticks after it fired.
A 200 6 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 cue.fire s:"V3XY5Z7B" s:"V3XY5Z7C"
A 300 7 engine run.failed s:"V3XY5Z7C" s:"timeout"
